The '''Bishuo language''' is an extinct or nearly extinct [[southern Bantoid]] language of [[Cameroon]]. It was spoken in the [[North West Province]], [[Menchum|Menchum Department]], Furu-Awa Subdivision, Ntjieka, Furu-Turuwa and the Furu-Sambari villages. It was related to [[Bikya language|Bikya]]. It was reported by Breton 1986 that the [[Bishuo people]] had shifted to [[Jukun Takum language|Jukun]], with apparently only one remaining person, over 60 years old, who knew any Bishuo.{{cn|date=April 2026}}

Bruce Connell's survey of "seriously endangered" languages in Central Africa listed it as having one speaker, based on Breton's report.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Connell |first1=Bruce |title=Language Diversity Endangered |date=31 December 2008 |publisher=DE GRUYTER |isbn=978-3-11-017050-4 |pages=163–178 |doi=10.1515/9783110905694-010 |url=https://www.degruyterbrill.com/document/doi/10.1515/9783110905694/html |chapter-url=https://www.degruyterbrill.com/document/doi/10.1515/9783110905694-010/html |access-date=1 April 2026 |chapter=Endangered Languages in Central Africa}}</ref>{{rp|174}} A 2003 linguistic survey concluded that the last living Bishuo speaker died sometime between 1983 and 2002.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Kouega |first1=Jean-Paul |title=The Language Situation in Cameroon |date=2008 |publisher=Taylor & Francis |url=https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.2167/cilp110.0 |access-date=1 April 2026}}</ref>{{rp|76}}

